AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2023B", "label": "2023 B", "problems": [{"q": "<p> Mrs. Jones is pouring orange juice into four identical glasses for her four sons. She fills the first three glasses completely but runs out of juice when the fourth glass is only <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span> full. What fraction of a glass must Mrs. Jones pour from each of the first three glasses into the fourth glass so that all four glasses will have the same amount of juice?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{12}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{9}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Mrs. Jones has a total of <span class=\"tex\">3 \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{10}{3}</span> glasses of orange juice. Distributing the orange juice equally among the four glasses means that each glass receives <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{10}{3}=\\frac{5}{6}</span> of a glass of orange juice. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{C})\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}</span> of a glass of juice must be removed from each of the first three glasses.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> Carlos went to a sports store to buy running shoes. Running shoes were on sale, with prices reduced by <span class=\"tex\">20 \\%</span> on every pair of shoes. Carlos also knew that he had to pay a <span class=\"tex\">7.5 \\%</span> sales tax on the discounted price. He had <span class=\"tex\">\\$ 43</span>. What is the original (before discount) price of the most expensive shoes he could afford to buy?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\$ 46</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\$ 50</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\$ 48</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\$ 47</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\$ 49</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> represent the original price of the shoes in dollars. The discounted price is <span class=\"tex\">0.8 x</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">1.075 \\times 0.8 x=0.86 x</span> is the total amount Carlos has to pay. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">0.86 x</span> must be less than or equal to 43 , so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x \\leq 43 \\div 0.86=50</span></p><p>The original price of the most expensive shoes he could afford to buy is <span class=\"tex\">\\$ (\\text{B})\\boxed{50}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> A <span class=\"tex\">3-4-5</span> right triangle is inscribed in circle <span class=\"tex\">A</span>, and a <span class=\"tex\">5-12-13</span> right triangle is inscribed in circle <span class=\"tex\">B</span>. What is the ratio of the area of circle <span class=\"tex\">A</span> to the area of circle <span class=\"tex\">B?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{25}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{9}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{25}{169}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{4}{25}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>The hypotenuse of a right triangle is a diameter of its circumscribed circle. Thus the diameter of circle <span class=\"tex\">A</span> is 5 , and the diameter of circle <span class=\"tex\">B</span> is 13 . Hence the requested ratio is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{\\pi\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2}}{\\pi\\left(\\frac{13}{2}\\right)^{2}}=(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{25}{169}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> Jackson's paintbrush makes a narrow strip with a width of <span class=\"tex\">6.5</span> millimeters. Jackson has enough paint to make a strip <span class=\"tex\">25</span> meters long. How many square centimeters of paper could Jackson cover with paint?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">162,500</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">162.5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1,625</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1,625,000</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16250</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Convert both measurements to centimeters. Because 1 millimeter is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{10}</span> of a centimeter, the width of the line is <span class=\"tex\">0.65=6.5 \\times 10^{-1} \\mathrm{~cm}</span>. Because a meter is 100 centimeters, the length of the line is <span class=\"tex\">2,500=2.5 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~cm}</span>. The total area is therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(6.5 \\times 10^{-1}\\right)\\left(2.5 \\times 10^{3}\\right)=16.25 \\times 10^{2}=(\\text{C})\\boxed{1,625 \\text { square centimeters. }}</span></p><p>Note: This is about 2.7 sheets of letter-sized paper.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> You are playing a game. A <span class=\"tex\">2 \\times 1</span> rectangle covers two adjacent squares (oriented either horizontally or vertically) of a <span class=\"tex\">3 \\times 3</span> grid of squares, but you are not told which two squares are covered. Your goal is to find at least one square that is covered by the rectangle. A \"turn\" consists of you guessing a square, after which you are told whether that square is covered by the hidden rectangle. What is the minimum number of turns you need to ensure that at least one of your guessed squares is covered by the rectangle?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Any <span class=\"tex\">2 \\times 1</span> rectangle must cover one of the shaded squares in the figure below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-088.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Therefore guessing these <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{C})\\boxed{4}</span> shaded squares will always result in finding a square covered by the rectangle.</p>\n<p>It remains to show that 3 guesses are not always sufficient. If any 2 of the 3 guessed squares are in the same row (or column), then <span class=\"tex\">2 \\times 1</span> rectangles in the unselected row (or column) will not cover any of the 3 guessed squares. So if only 3 guesses are made, one square from each row and each column must be selected. There are two ways this can happen, up to symmetry, shown below. A <span class=\"tex\">2 \\times 1</span> rectangle covering two unshaded squares can be found in each diagram, so 3 guesses are not sufficient in general.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-088_1_.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> When the roots of the polynomial</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(x)=(x-1)^{1}(x-2)^{2}(x-3)^{3} \\cdots(x-10)^{10}</span></p><p>are removed from the real number line, what remains is the union of <span class=\"tex\">11</span> disjoint open intervals. On how many of these intervals is <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> positive?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Note that the function <span class=\"tex\">P(x)&gt;0</span> for <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;10</span>. Moving right to left along the real number line, <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> changes sign precisely when passing through a root of odd multiplicity. This means that the function changes sign as <span class=\"tex\">x</span> passes through <span class=\"tex\">9,7,5,3</span>, and 1 , and its value is 0 at <span class=\"tex\">10,8,6,4</span>, and 2 as well. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">P(x)&gt;0</span> on the intervals <span class=\"tex\">(10, \\infty),(9,10),(6,7),(5,6),(2,3)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(1,2)</span>, a total of <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{C})\\boxed{6}</span> intervals.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-089.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Perfect square factors are always nonnegative, so they can be removed without changing the signs. Therefore, for any <span class=\"tex\">x</span> with <span class=\"tex\">P(x) \\neq 0</span>, the polynomial</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">Q(x)=(x-1)(x-3)(x-5)(x-7)(x-9)</span></p><p>will have the same sign as <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span>. Now <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span> is positive on the following intervals: <span class=\"tex\">(1,2)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(2,3)</span> (where four of the factors are negative), <span class=\"tex\">(5,6)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(6,7)</span> (where two of the factors are negative), and <span class=\"tex\">(9,10)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(10, \\infty)</span> (where all the factors are positive). This gives a total of <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{C})\\boxed{6}</span> intervals on which <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)&gt;0</span>. On the other 5 intervals there are an odd number of negative factors, so <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span>, and therefore <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> as well, is negative.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> For how many integers <span class=\"tex\">n</span> does the expression</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\log \\left(n^{2}\\right)-(\\log n)^{2}}{\\log n-3}}</span></p><p>represent a real number, where log denotes the base <span class=\"tex\">10</span> logarithm?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">900</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">902</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">901</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>First note that the fraction under the square root will equal 0 when <span class=\"tex\">n=1</span>. There are now 2 cases to consider: Either <span class=\"tex\">\\log n&gt;3</span> or <span class=\"tex\">\\log n&lt;3</span>. In the first case, <span class=\"tex\">\\log n&gt;3</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">n&gt;1000</span>, so the numerator <span class=\"tex\">\\log \\left(n^{2}\\right)-(\\log n)^{2}=2 \\log n-(\\log n)^{2}&lt;0</span> because <span class=\"tex\">2 \\log n&lt;(\\log n)^{2}</span> whenever <span class=\"tex\">\\log n&gt;2</span>. Thus the fraction under the square root will be negative and there are no solutions in this case.</p>\n<p>For the second case, <span class=\"tex\">\\log n&lt;3</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">n&lt;1000</span>. The fraction under the square root will be nonnegative provided <span class=\"tex\">\\log \\left(n^{2}\\right)-(\\log n)^{2} \\leq 0</span>. This will occur when <span class=\"tex\">\\log n \\geq 2</span>, i.e., when <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 100</span>. Thus the given expression will evaluate to a real number for any integer <span class=\"tex\">n</span> in the range <span class=\"tex\">100 \\leq n&lt;1000</span>. Together with the <span class=\"tex\">n=1</span> solution, this gives a total of <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{901}</span> integers for which the expression evaluates to a real number.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>If the expression represents a real number, then <span class=\"tex\">\\log n</span> is defined, so <span class=\"tex\">n&gt;0</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">n</span> is an integer, <span class=\"tex\">n&gt;0</span> implies <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 1</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">m=\\log n \\geq \\log 1=0</span>. The fraction under the square root can be expressed as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{2 m-m^{2}}{m-3}=\\frac{m(2-m)}{m-3}</span></p><p>This expression is meaningful and nonnegative when <span class=\"tex\">m=0</span> or <span class=\"tex\">2 \\leq m&lt;3</span>, as shown in the table below. If <span class=\"tex\">m=0</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">n=1</span>. When <span class=\"tex\">2 \\leq m&lt;3</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">100 \\leq n&lt;1000</span> and hence another 900 integral values of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> make the expression represent a real number. This gives a total of <span class=\"tex\">1+900=(\\text{E})\\boxed{901}</span> possible values of <span class=\"tex\">n</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}\n&amp; m &amp; 2-m &amp; m-3 &amp; \\frac{m(2-m)}{m-3} \\\\\n\\hline\nm=0 &amp; 0 &amp; + &amp; - &amp; 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n0&lt;m&lt;2 &amp; + &amp; + &amp; - &amp; - \\\\\n\\hline\nm=2 &amp; + &amp; 0 &amp; - &amp; 0 \\\\\n\\hline\n2&lt;m&lt;3 &amp; - &amp; - &amp; - &amp; + \\\\\n\\hline\nm=3 &amp; + &amp; - &amp; 0 &amp; undefined \\\\\n\\hline\nm&gt;3 &amp; + &amp; - &amp; + &amp; - \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> How many nonempty subsets <span class=\"tex\">B</span> of <span class=\"tex\">\\{0,1,2,3, \\ldots, 12\\}</span> have the property that the number of elements in <span class=\"tex\">B</span> is equal to the least element of <span class=\"tex\">B?</span> For example, <span class=\"tex\">B=\\{4,6,8,11\\}</span> satisfies the condition.</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">256</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">136</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">108</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">144</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">156</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Any nonempty subset satisfying this property with minimum value <span class=\"tex\">k</span> must contain <span class=\"tex\">k-1</span> elements greater than <span class=\"tex\">k</span>. This implies that <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq 6</span>, and there are <span class=\"tex\">\\binom{12-k}{k-1}</span> such subsets. The total number of subsets with the required property is therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\binom{6}{5}+\\binom{7}{4}+\\binom{8}{3}+\\binom{9}{2}+\\binom{10}{1}+\\binom{11}{0}=6+35+56+36+10+1=(\\text{D})\\boxed{144} .</span></p><p>Note: This problem illustrates an identity relating the sum of the elements on a \"shallow\" diagonal in Pascal's triangle to the Fibonacci numbers.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> What is the area of the region in the coordinate plane defined by</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">|| x|-1|+|| y|-1| \\leq 1 ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Replacing <span class=\"tex\">x</span> by <span class=\"tex\">-x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> by <span class=\"tex\">-y</span> does not change the inequality, so the region is symmetric with respect to both coordinate axes. In the first quadrant the given inequality is equivalent to <span class=\"tex\">|x-1|+|y-1| \\leq 1</span>, and its graph is the square bounded by the lines <span class=\"tex\">x+y=1, x+y=3</span>, <span class=\"tex\">x-y=-1</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">x-y=1</span> together with its interior. The area of this square is 2 . Therefore the area of the entire region is <span class=\"tex\">2 \\cdot 4=(\\text{B})\\boxed{8}</span>. (The region consists of the 4 shaded squares in the figure below.)</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-090.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> In the <span class=\"tex\">x y</span>-plane, a circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">4</span> with center on the positive <span class=\"tex\">x</span> axis is tangent to the <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis at the origin, and a circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">10</span> with center on the positive <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis is tangent to the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis at the origin. What is the slope of the line passing through the two points at which these circles intersect?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{5}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>In rectangular coordinates, the equations of the circles are <span class=\"tex\">(x-4)^{2}+y^{2}=4^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+(y-10)^{2}=10^{2}</span>, respectively. Subtracting the two equations and simplifying yields <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{2}{5} x</span>. Any point lying on both circles must satisfy this equation, which must therefore be the equation of the line through the two points of intersection. The requested slope is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{2}{5}}</span>. The second intersection point can be found by solving the system of equations, and this point is <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{200}{29}, \\frac{80}{29}\\right)</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-091.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>In polar coordinates, the equations of the circles are <span class=\"tex\">r=4 \\cos \\theta</span> and <span class=\"tex\">r=10 \\sin \\theta</span>, respectively. The circles intersect at the origin and at the point where <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cos \\theta=10 \\sin \\theta</span>. In the second case, this equation implies that <span class=\"tex\">\\tan \\theta=\\frac{2}{5}</span>. The requested slope is therefore <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{2}{5}}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">A(4,0)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C(0,10)</span> be the centers of the two circles, and let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B</span> be their intersection points. Because <span class=\"tex\">C O=C B</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A O=A B</span>, the diagonals of kite <span class=\"tex\">O A B C</span> are perpendicular. The slope of <span class=\"tex\">A C</span> is <span class=\"tex\">-\\frac{5}{2}</span>, and the slope of <span class=\"tex\">O B</span> is the negative reciprocal of this value, namely <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{2}{5}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> What is the maximum area of an isosceles trapezoid that has legs of length <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and one base twice as long as the other?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{8}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{4}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let the bases have lengths <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 x</span>. Extend the legs of the isosceles trapezoid to form two isosceles triangles with vertex angle <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span>, as shown. with vertex angle <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span>, as shown.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-092_1_.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Because the ratio of the bases is <span class=\"tex\">1: 2</span>, the two congruent sides of the smaller isosceles triangle have length 1 . The area of the trapezoid is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{3}{4}</span> the area of the large isosceles triangle, so its area is maximized when the area of the large isosceles triangle is maximized. The area of the large isosceles triangle as a function of <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{2} \\cdot \\sin \\theta=2 \\sin \\theta</span>. The maximum area of this isosceles triangle is 2 , occurring when <span class=\"tex\">\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}</span>. Therefore the maximum area of the trapezoid is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{3}{4} \\cdot 2=(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">h</span> be the height of the trapezoid, let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 x</span> be the base lengths, and let <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha</span> be the base angle, as shown.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-092.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Then <span class=\"tex\">\\sin \\alpha=h</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 \\cos \\alpha=x</span>. The area of the trapezoid is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{x+2 x}{2} \\cdot h</span>, which can be written as a function of <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha</span> :</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">3 \\cos \\alpha \\cdot \\sin \\alpha=\\frac{3}{2} \\sin (2 \\alpha) .</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (2 \\alpha) \\leq 1</span>, the maximum area is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}</span>, which occurs when <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (2 \\alpha)=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha=\\frac{\\pi}{4}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">h</span> be the height of the trapezoid, and let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 x</span> be the base lengths (see the figure for the second solution). Then <span class=\"tex\">1^{2}=h^{2}+\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}</span>, so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">h=\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}}</span></p><p>The area <span class=\"tex\">A</span> of the trapezoid is\\<br/>\nso</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A=\\frac{x+2 x}{2} \\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}}</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">A^{2}=\\frac{9 x^{2}}{4}\\left(1-\\frac{x^{2}}{4}\\right)=\\frac{9 x^{2}}{4}-\\frac{9 x^{4}}{16} .</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">u=x^{2}</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">A^{2}=-\\frac{9}{16} u^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} u</span>, which has a maximum when</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">u=\\frac{-\\frac{9}{4}}{2 \\cdot\\left(-\\frac{9}{16}\\right)}=2</span></p><p>Substituting <span class=\"tex\">u=2</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">A^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A=(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> For complex numbers <span class=\"tex\">u=a+b i</span> and <span class=\"tex\">v=c+d i</span> (where <span class=\"tex\">i=\\sqrt{-1}</span> ), define the binary operation</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">u \\otimes v=a c+b d i</span></p><p>Suppose <span class=\"tex\">z</span> is a complex number such that <span class=\"tex\">z \\otimes z=z^{2}+40</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">|z|?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{10}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5 \\sqrt{2}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">z=a+b i</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">z \\otimes z=a^{2}+b^{2} i</span> and <span class=\"tex\">z^{2}=\\left(a^{2}-b^{2}\\right)+2 a b i</span>, so the original equation is equivalent to</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a^{2}+b^{2} i=\\left(a^{2}-b^{2}+40\\right)+2 a b i</span></p><p>Setting the real parts of both sides equal gives <span class=\"tex\">b^{2}=40</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">b= \\pm 2 \\sqrt{10}</span>. Setting the imaginary parts of both sides equal yields <span class=\"tex\">a=\\frac{b}{2}= \\pm \\sqrt{10}</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">|z|=\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}}=\\sqrt{10+40}=(\\text{E})\\boxed{5 \\sqrt{2}}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> A rectangular box <span class=\"tex\">\\mathcal{P}</span> has distinct edge lengths <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span>. The sum of the lengths of all <span class=\"tex\">12</span> edges of <span class=\"tex\">\\mathcal{P}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">13</span> the sum of the areas of all <span class=\"tex\">6</span> faces of <span class=\"tex\">\\mathcal{P}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">11 / 2</span>, and the volume of <span class=\"tex\">\\mathcal{P}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">1 / 2</span>. What is the length of the longest interior diagonal connecting two vertices of <span class=\"tex\">\\mathcal{P}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{9}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{2}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>The longest diagonal passes through the interior of the box, connecting opposite vertices; its length is <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">4 a+4 b+4 c=13</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 a b+2 b c+2 a c=\\frac{11}{2}</span>, it follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(\\frac{13}{4}\\right)^{2}=(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2 a b+2 b c+2 a c=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\\frac{11}{2}</span></p><p>Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=\\left(\\frac{13}{4}\\right)^{2}-\\frac{11}{2}=\\frac{81}{16}</span></p><p>The longest diagonal has length <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}=(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{9}{4}}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: Such a box <span class=\"tex\">\\mathcal{P}</span> exists with edge lengths <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{4}, 1</span>, and 2 . The volume is not needed to solve this problem.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> For how many ordered pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> of integers does the polynomial <span class=\"tex\">x^{3}+a x^{2}+b x+6</span> have <span class=\"tex\">3</span> distinct integer roots?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">r, s</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">t</span> be the roots of <span class=\"tex\">x^{3}+a x^{2}+b x+6</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x^{3}+a x^{2}+b x+6=(x-r)(x-s)(x-t)=x^{3}-(r+s+t) x^{2}+(r s+s t+t r) x-r s t</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">r s t=-6, r+s+t=-a</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">r s+s t+t r=b</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">r, s</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">t</span> are distinct integers and <span class=\"tex\">r s t=-6</span>, the possible roots <span class=\"tex\">\\{r, s, t\\}</span> are the sets <span class=\"tex\">\\{-1,1,6\\},\\{-1,2,3\\},\\{1,-2,3\\},\\{1,2,-3\\}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\{-1,-2,-3\\}</span>. The corresponding ordered pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> are <span class=\"tex\">(-6,-1),(-4,1),(-2,-5),(0,-7)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(6,11)</span>, respectively. This gives <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{A})\\boxed{5}</span> possible ordered pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> are positive integers such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{a}{14}+\\dfrac{b}{15}=\\dfrac{c}{210}</span></p><p>Which of the following statements are necessarily true?</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">I</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, 14)=1</span> or <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(b, 15)=1</span> or both, then <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, 210)=1</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">II</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, 210)=1</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, 14)=1</span> or <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(b, 15)=1</span> or both.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">III</span>. <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, 210)=1</span> if and only if <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, 14)=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd (b, 15)}=1</span>.</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">I, II</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">III</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">I</span> only", "<span class=\"tex\">I</span> and <span class=\"tex\">II</span> only", "<span class=\"tex\">III</span> only", "<span class=\"tex\">II</span> and <span class=\"tex\">III</span> only"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Note that 14 and 15 are relatively prime and <span class=\"tex\">210=14 \\cdot 15</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">q</span> and <span class=\"tex\">r</span> be two relatively prime positive integers (such as 14 and 15). The equation <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{a}{q}+\\frac{b}{r}=\\frac{c}{q r}</span> is equivalent to <span class=\"tex\">a r+b q=c</span>. Hence</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, q)=\\operatorname{gcd}(a r+b q, q)=\\operatorname{gcd}(a r, q)=\\operatorname{gcd}(a, q)</span></p><p>Similarly, <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, r)=\\operatorname{gcd}(b, r)</span>. Also, <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, q r)=1</span> if and only if <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, q)=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(c, r)=1</span>. Therefore Statement III is true, and that statement logically implies Statement II. To see that Statement I might be false, let <span class=\"tex\">a=1, b=3</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c=57</span>; then the hypothesis is true but the conclusion is false. Thus Statements <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{II and III}</span> are the only ones that are necessarily true.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> In the state of Coinland, coins have values of <span class=\"tex\">6,10</span> and <span class=\"tex\">15</span> cents. Suppose <span class=\"tex\">x</span> is the value in cents of the most expensive item in Coinland that cannot be purchased using these coins with exact change. What is the sum of the digits of <span class=\"tex\">x?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>The problem is asking for the sum of the digits of the greatest integer <span class=\"tex\">x</span> that cannot be written in the form <span class=\"tex\">6 a+10 b+15 c</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> are nonnegative integers.. If <span class=\"tex\">c=0</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">6 a+10 b+15 c</span> is a multiple of 2 . If <span class=\"tex\">b=0</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">6 a+10 b+15 c</span> is a multiple of 3 . If <span class=\"tex\">a=0</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">6 a+10 b+15 c</span> is a multiple of 5 . If none of <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">c</span> equals 0 , then <span class=\"tex\">6 a+10 b+15 c \\geq 6+10+15=31</span>. Therefore prime numbers less than 31 cannot be written in the required form. In particular, 29 cannot be written in this form.</p>\n<p>To show that 29 is the greatest positive integer that cannot be written as such a sum, observe that the following six consecutive integers can be written in this way.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; 30=15+15 \\\\\n&amp; 31=6+10+15 \\\\\n&amp; 32=6+6+10+10 \\\\\n&amp; 33=6+6+6+15 \\\\\n&amp; 34=6+6+6+6+10 \\\\\n&amp; 35=10+10+15\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Because additional 6 s can be added to each of these sums, it follows that all positive integers greater than 29 can be written in the required form. Therefore the most expensive item in Coinland that cannot be purchased using coins of the given denominations has value <span class=\"tex\">x=29</span>, and the requested digit sum is <span class=\"tex\">2+9=(\\text{D})\\boxed{11}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> The three side lengths of a triangle are in arithmetic progression with shortest side of length <span class=\"tex\">6</span> One of the interior angles measures <span class=\"tex\">120^{\\circ}</span>. What is the area of the triangle?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">12 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8 \\sqrt{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20 \\sqrt{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15 \\sqrt{3}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">d</span> be positive real numbers such that the side lengths are <span class=\"tex\">a-d</span>, <span class=\"tex\">a</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a+d</span>. Then the longest side, that of length <span class=\"tex\">a+d</span>, must be opposite the <span class=\"tex\">120^{\\circ}</span> angle. By the Law of Cosines,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(a+d)^{2}=a^{2}+(a-d)^{2}-2 a(a-d) \\cos 120^{\\circ} .</span></p><p>Simplifying yields <span class=\"tex\">2 a^{2}-5 a d=0</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">a(2 a-5 d)=0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">d=\\frac{2}{5} a</span>. Therefore the three sides have lengths <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{3}{5} a, a, \\frac{7}{5} a</span>. Because the shortest side has length 6 , the three side lengths are <span class=\"tex\">6,10,14</span>.\\<br/>\nThe area of the triangle is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 10 \\cdot \\sin 120^{\\circ}=30 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=(\\text{E})\\boxed{15 \\sqrt{3}} .</span></p><p>Alternatively, one can use Heron's Formula with <span class=\"tex\">s=\\frac{6+10+14}{2}=15</span> to find the area.</p>\n<p>Note: A triple of integers that are the side lengths of a triangle with exactly one <span class=\"tex\">60^{\\circ}</span> angle is called an Eisenstein triple, similar to a Pythagorean triple for a triangle with a <span class=\"tex\">90^{\\circ}</span> angle. Some examples of such triples are <span class=\"tex\">(3,7,8)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(8,13,15)</span>. Similarly, there are other triangles with integer side lengths and a <span class=\"tex\">120^{\\circ}</span> angle, such as <span class=\"tex\">(7,8,13)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(5,16,19)</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> Last academic year Yolanda and Zelda took different courses that did not necessarily administer the same number of quizzes during each of the two semesters. Yolanda's average on all the quizzes she took during the first semester was <span class=\"tex\">3</span> points higher than Zelda's average on all the quizzes she took during the first semester. Yolanda's average on all the quizzes she took during the second semester was <span class=\"tex\">18</span> points higher than her average for the first semester and was again <span class=\"tex\">3</span> points higher than Zelda's average on all the quizzes Zelda took during her second semester. Which one of the following statements cannot possibly be true?</p>", "c": ["Yolanda's quiz average for the academic year was <span class=\"tex\">22</span> points higher than Zelda's.", "Zelda's quiz average for the academic year was higher than Yolanda's.", "Yolanda's quiz average for the academic year was <span class=\"tex\">3</span> points higher than Zelda's.", "Zelda's quiz average for the academic year equaled Yolanda's.", "If Zelda had scored <span class=\"tex\">3</span> points higher on each quiz she took, then she would have had the same average for the academic year as Yolanda."], "a": "A", "s": "<p>To see that choice (C) might be true, observe that Yolanda's average on quizzes for the whole academic year would have been 3 points higher than Zelda's if the number of quizzes in each course was the same each semester. And if all courses have the same number of quizzes, the same argument shows that choice ( <span class=\"tex\">\\mathbf{E}</span> ) is also possible.</p>\n<p>While counterintuitive, choice (B) could be true; this is an example of Simpson's Paradox. Indeed, suppose Yolanda's average was <span class=\"tex\">X</span> during her first semester. Then Yolanda's average was <span class=\"tex\">X+18</span> her second semester and Zelda's averages were <span class=\"tex\">X-3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">X+15</span> her two semesters. Suppose Yolanda took 8 quizzes her first semester and 2 quizzes her second semester, while Zelda took 2 quizzes her first semester and 8 quizzes her second semester. Then Yolanda's average for the whole academic year would have been</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{8 X+2(X+18)}{8+2}=X+3.6</span></p><p>and Zelda's would have been</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{2(X-3)+8(X+15)}{2+8}=X+11.4</span></p><p>so Zelda would have had the better average for the whole academic year. Choice (D) could be true in much the same way. Again suppose Yolanda took 8 quizzes her first semester and 2 quizzes her second semester, and suppose Zelda took <span class=\"tex\">a</span> quizzes her first semester and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> quizzes her second semester. Then their averages would both have been <span class=\"tex\">X+3.6</span> if and only if</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{a(X-3)+b(X+15)}{a+b}=X+3.6 .</span></p><p>This equation is equivalent to <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{11.4}{6.6}</span>, which will be true if <span class=\"tex\">a=114</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b=66</span> (or <span class=\"tex\">a=19</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b=11</span> ).</p>\n<p>This leaves choice (A) as the only possible answer to this problem. To see that Yolanda's average on quizzes for the whole academic year cannot have been 22 points higher than Zelda's, use the notation as above and observe that Yolanda's average for the whole academic year must have been between <span class=\"tex\">X</span> and <span class=\"tex\">X+18</span> and Zelda's must have been between <span class=\"tex\">X-3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">X+15</span>. The greatest possible difference in their averages for the academic year is therefore less than <span class=\"tex\">(X+18)-(X-3)=21</span> points and cannot be 22 points.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> Each of <span class=\"tex\">2023</span> balls is randomly placed into one of <span class=\"tex\">3</span> bins. Which of the following is closest to the probability that each of the bins will contain an odd number of balls?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{10}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{4}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>When an odd number of balls are dropped into three bins, the parities of the bins' contents are either odd-odd-odd or even-even-odd (in some order). Consider dropping the balls one at a time. Let <span class=\"tex\">P(n)</span> be the probability that each of the bins contains an odd number of balls (state odd-odd-odd) after <span class=\"tex\">2 n+1</span> balls have been deposited. Then <span class=\"tex\">P(0)=0</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">P(n+1)</span> can be computed from <span class=\"tex\">P(n)</span> by considering what happens with the next 2 balls. With probability <span class=\"tex\">P(n)</span> the state is currently odd-odd-odd, and there are 9 equally likely ways for 2 more balls to land. With probability <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}</span> they will land in the same bin and the new state will be odd-odd-odd. With probability <span class=\"tex\">1-P(n)</span> the state is currently even-even-odd, and 2 of the 9 possibilities for the next 2 balls will change the state to odd-odd-odd. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(n+1)=P(n) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+(1-P(n)) \\cdot \\frac{2}{9},</span></p><p>which simplifies to</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(n+1)=\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot P(n)+\\frac{2}{9} .</span></p><p>This linear recurrence can be solved by assuming that <span class=\"tex\">P(n)=a \\cdot r^{n}+b</span> for some constants <span class=\"tex\">r, a</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">P(0)=0</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">a \\cdot r^{0}+b=0</span>. Because</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(1)=\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot P(0)+\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 0+\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{2}{9}</span></p><p>it follows that <span class=\"tex\">a \\cdot r^{1}+b=\\frac{2}{9}</span>. Because</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(2)=\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot P(1)+\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}=\\frac{20}{81}</span></p><p>it follows that <span class=\"tex\">a \\cdot r^{2}+b=\\frac{20}{81}</span>. Solving this system of equations gives <span class=\"tex\">r=\\frac{1}{9}, a=-\\frac{1}{4}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">b=\\frac{1}{4}</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(n)=-\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)^{n}+\\frac{1}{4}</span></p><p>The recurrence and initial condition are satisfied by this formula for <span class=\"tex\">P(n)</span>, so the assumption about the form of <span class=\"tex\">P(n)</span> was justified. The problem asks for <span class=\"tex\">P(1011)</span>, which nearly equals <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}</span> because the first term is very close to 0 .</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> and <span class=\"tex\">E</span> represent parities odd and even. Because the number of balls is odd, there are only 4 possible parity states of the three bins: <span class=\"tex\">E E O, E O E, O E E</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">O O O</span>. The problem asks for the approximate probability that the state is <span class=\"tex\">O O O</span>. If the number of balls is sufficiently large, the probability that the first bin has an even number of balls is very nearly <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}</span>. The same holds for the second bin. Thus <span class=\"tex\">E E, E O, O E</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">O O</span> are all approximately equally likely for the parities of the first two bins. The parity of the third bin is completely determined by the parities of the first two bins because the total number of balls is odd. Thus the probability of each of the 4 parity states is about <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>If the balls are placed randomly into the bins one at a time, after <span class=\"tex\">n</span> balls are placed, either</p>\n<ul>\n<li>all three bins contain the same parity of balls (that is, either all three bins contain an odd number of balls or all three bins contain an even number of balls),<br/>\nor</li>\n<li>two of the bins contain one parity of balls and the third bin contains the other (that is, either two bins have an an odd number of balls and the remaining bin has an even number of balls, or two bins contain an even number of balls and remaining bin has an odd number of balls).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note that if all three bins currently contain the same parity of balls, they will surely not contain the same parity once another ball is added to any bin. However, if two bins currently contain the same parity of balls and the third bin contains the other parity, a ball added to the third bin will make all three bins contain the same parity, but one added to either of the first two bins will result in two of the bins having the same parity but not the remaining bin.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">p_{n}</span> represent the probability that all three bins contain the same parity of balls. From the reasoning above, <span class=\"tex\">p_{n+1}=0 \\cdot p_{n}+\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1-p_{n}\\right)=\\frac{1-p_{n}}{3}</span> Assuming that <span class=\"tex\">p_{n}</span> approaches a limit <span class=\"tex\">L</span> as <span class=\"tex\">n \\rightarrow \\infty</span>, the recurrence implies that <span class=\"tex\">L=\\frac{1-L}{3}</span>, from which it follows that <span class=\"tex\">L=\\frac{1}{4}</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">p_{2023}</span> should be close to <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Cyrus the frog sits on a flat surface. He jumps, landing <span class=\"tex\">2</span> feet away. He then chooses a direction at random and again jumps <span class=\"tex\">2</span> feet. What is the probability that after the second jump Cyrus lands within <span class=\"tex\">1</span> foot of his starting position?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{6}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\arctan \\dfrac{1}{2}}{\\pi}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2 \\arcsin \\dfrac{1}{4}}{\\pi}</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">A</span> be the starting position, and let <span class=\"tex\">B</span> be Cyrus's position after the first jump. Let <span class=\"tex\">C_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C_{2}</span> be the points at which the circle of radius 2 centered at <span class=\"tex\">B</span> (which consists of all of Cyrus's possible positions after the second jump) intersects the circle of radius 1 centered at <span class=\"tex\">A</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-098.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Cyrus will land within 1 foot of his starting position after the second jump if and only if he lands on minor arc <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{C_{1} C_{2}}</span>. Therefore the requested probability is the fraction of the perimeter of the circle occupied by this arc. Let <span class=\"tex\">M</span> be the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C_{1}}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C_{1}</span> is isosceles, <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B M C_{1}</span> is a right angle.</p>\n<p>Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sin \\left(\\angle M B C_{1}\\right)=\\frac{M C_{1}}{B C_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}</span></p><p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle M B C_{1}=\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\angle C_{1} B C_{2}</span>. The fraction of the perimeter occupied by minor arc <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{C_{1} C_{2}}</span> and the requested probability is therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{4 \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}}{2 \\pi}=(\\text{E})\\boxed{\\frac{2 \\arcsin \\frac{1}{4}}{\\pi}}</span></p><p>This value is about 0.161 , slightly less than <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{6}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> A lampshade is made in the form of the lateral surface of the frustum of a right circular cone. The height of the frustum is <span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{3}</span> inches, its top diameter is <span class=\"tex\">6</span> inches, and its bottom diameter is <span class=\"tex\">12</span> inches. A bug is at the bottom of the lampshade and there is a glob of honey on the top edge of the lampshade at the spot farthest from the bug. The bug wants to crawl to the honey, but it must stay on the surface of the lampshade. What is the length in inches of its shortest path to the honey?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2024_07_16_f25eab1d78104df662c3g-05.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">6+3 \\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6+6 \\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{3}+\\pi</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>The circumference of the bottom of the cone is <span class=\"tex\">12 \\pi</span>, the height of the (full) cone is <span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{3}</span>, and the slant height of the cone is 12 .</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-099_2_.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>When the frustum is slit along the slant height and rolled out (into a sector of an annulus), the outer radius will be 12 and the arc length will be <span class=\"tex\">12 \\pi</span>, making the measure of the arc <span class=\"tex\">180^{\\circ}</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-099.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Refer to the unrolled figure. The bug wants to crawl from point <span class=\"tex\">B</span> to point <span class=\"tex\">H</span>. Note that because <span class=\"tex\">H</span> is at the top of the inner semicircle and <span class=\"tex\">B</span> is to the right of its center, <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B H}</span> passes through the interior of the semicircle and thus does not remain on the surface of the lampshade, as required. Therefore the bug will need to crawl from <span class=\"tex\">B</span> to <span class=\"tex\">D</span> (where <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span> is tangent to the inner circle), then along the\\<br/>\nsemicircle to <span class=\"tex\">H</span>. The inside radius is 6 , so <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B C D</span> is a <span class=\"tex\">30-60-90^{\\circ}</span> right triangle and <span class=\"tex\">B D=6 \\sqrt{3}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{H C} \\perp \\overline{B C}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B C D=60^{\\circ}</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\angle H C D=30^{\\circ}</span>. Therefore the length of <span class=\"tex\">\\widehat{D H}</span> is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{30^{\\circ}}{360^{\\circ}} \\cdot 2 \\pi \\cdot 6=\\pi</span></p><p>The length of the path from <span class=\"tex\">B</span> to <span class=\"tex\">D</span> to <span class=\"tex\">H</span> is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{6 \\sqrt{3}+\\pi}</span> inches.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> A real-valued function <span class=\"tex\">f</span> has the property that for all real numbers <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(a+b)+f(a-b)=2 f(a) f(b)</span></p><p>Which one of the following cannot be the value of <span class=\"tex\">f(1)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-2</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>First note that if <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=0</span> for all <span class=\"tex\">x</span>, then the defining identity is met, and <span class=\"tex\">f(1)=0</span>, eliminating option (A). Now assume that <span class=\"tex\">f</span> is not identically zero. Setting <span class=\"tex\">b=0</span> and choosing <span class=\"tex\">a</span> so that <span class=\"tex\">f(a) \\neq 0</span>, the identity reduces to <span class=\"tex\">f(a)+f(a)=2 f(a) f(0)</span>, yielding <span class=\"tex\">f(0)=1</span>. Setting <span class=\"tex\">a=b=\\frac{1}{2}</span> and using <span class=\"tex\">f(0)=1</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">f(1)+1=2 f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">f(1)=-2</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">-2+1=</span> <span class=\"tex\">2\\left[f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right]^{2}</span>, which is impossible. So <span class=\"tex\">f(1)</span> cannot equal <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{-2}</span> , and the correct answer is (E).</p>\n<p>For completeness, it remains to show why options (C), (B) and (D) are attainable as <span class=\"tex\">f(1)</span>. Note that the cosine function satisfies the defining identity, as does the hyperbolic cosine function, and more generally so do <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (k x)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\cosh (k x)</span>. Letting <span class=\"tex\">k</span> equal <span class=\"tex\">\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{2}</span>, and 0 in <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (k x)</span>, respectively, shows that options (C), (A), and <span class=\"tex\">(\\mathbf{B})</span> are possible values of <span class=\"tex\">f(1)</span>. The range of the continuous function <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=\\cosh (k x)</span> is the interval <span class=\"tex\">[1, \\infty)</span>, which includes 2 . Therefore there must be a value of <span class=\"tex\">k</span> that makes <span class=\"tex\">f(1)=2</span>, eliminating option (D).</p>\n<p>Note: While not required for this problem, it is possible to determine a specific <span class=\"tex\">x</span> satisfying <span class=\"tex\">\\cosh x=</span> 2. Observe that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\cosh (\\ln (2+\\sqrt{3})) &amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot\\left(e^{\\ln (2+\\sqrt{3})}+e^{-\\ln (2+\\sqrt{3})}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot\\left(2+\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{1}{(2+\\sqrt{3})}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot\\left(2+\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{2-\\sqrt{3}}{4-3}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot(2+\\sqrt{3}+2-\\sqrt{3})=2\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">f(1)=2</span> when <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=\\cosh ((\\ln (2+\\sqrt{3})) x)</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> When a standard <span class=\"tex\">6</span>-sided die is rolled <span class=\"tex\">n</span> times, the product of the <span class=\"tex\">n</span> numbers rolled can be any of <span class=\"tex\">936</span> possible values. What is <span class=\"tex\">n?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>The product of the results of the rolls takes the form <span class=\"tex\">2^{a} 3^{b} 5^{c}</span>, and every roll contributes at most two prime factors to the product. No roll contributes more than one factor of 5 , so <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq c \\leq n</span>.</p>\n<p>No roll contributes more than one factor of 3 , and no roll can contribute both a 3 and a 5 , so <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq b \\leq</span> <span class=\"tex\">n-c</span>. No roll contributes more than two factors of 2 , no roll that contributes a 3 can contribute more than one factor of 2 , and no roll can contribute both a 2 and a 5 , so <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq a \\leq 2 n-b-2 c</span>. Each ordered triple <span class=\"tex\">(a, b, c)</span> determines a different product, so the number of possible products is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{c=0}^{n} \\sum_{b=0}^{n-c}(2 n-b-2 c+1) &amp; =\\sum_{c=0}^{n}\\left(\\sum_{b=0}^{n-c}(2 n-2 c+1)-\\sum_{b=0}^{n-c} b\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\sum_{c=0}^{n}\\left((2(n-c)+1)(n-c+1)-\\frac{1}{2}(n-c)(n-c+1)\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\sum_{c=0}^{n}\\left((2 c+1)(c+1)-\\frac{1}{2} c(c+1)\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\sum_{c=0}^{n}\\left(\\frac{3}{2} c^{2}+\\frac{5}{2} c+1\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}+\\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+(n+1) \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(n+2)}{2}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">936=\\frac{12^{2} \\cdot 13}{2}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">n=(\\text{A})\\boxed{11}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">a, b, c</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">d</span> are positive integers satisfying all of the following relations.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\na b c d &amp;= 2^{6} \\cdot 3^{9} \\cdot 5^{7} \\\\\n\\operatorname{lcm}(a, b) &amp;= 2^{3} \\cdot 3^{2} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n\\operatorname{lcm}(a, c) &amp;= 2^{3} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n\\operatorname{lcm}(a, d) &amp;= 2^{3} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n\\operatorname{lcm}(b, c) &amp;= 2^{1} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{2} \\\\\n\\operatorname{lcm}(b, d) &amp;= 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{2} \\\\\n\\operatorname{lcm}(c, d) &amp;= 2^{2} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{2}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>What is <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c, d)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">30</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">45</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(b, c)</span> contains just 1 factor of 2 , neither <span class=\"tex\">b</span> nor <span class=\"tex\">c</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">2^{2}</span>. However, <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(b, d)</span> contains 2 factors of 2 , and <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(a, b)</span> contains 3 factors, so <span class=\"tex\">d</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">2^{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">2^{3}</span>. Because the product <span class=\"tex\">a b c d</span> contains 6 factors of 2 , it follows that one of <span class=\"tex\">b</span> or <span class=\"tex\">c</span> is divisible by 2 , and the other is not. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c, d)</span> is not divisible by 2.</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(a, b)</span> contains just 2 factors of 3 , at least one of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">3^{2}</span>, but neither is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">3^{3}</span>. However, both <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(a, c)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(a, d)</span> contain 3 factors of 3 , so both <span class=\"tex\">c</span> and <span class=\"tex\">d</span> are divisible by <span class=\"tex\">3^{3}</span>. Because the product <span class=\"tex\">a b c d</span> contains 9 factors of 3, it follows that one of <span class=\"tex\">a</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">3^{2}</span>, and the other is divisible by 3 but not by <span class=\"tex\">3^{2}</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c, d)</span> is divisible by 3 but not by <span class=\"tex\">3^{2}</span>.</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">1 \\mathrm{~cm}(b, c), \\operatorname{lcm}(b, d)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(c, d)</span> each contain just 2 factors of 5, none of <span class=\"tex\">b, c</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">d</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">5^{3}</span>, but at least two of them are divisible by <span class=\"tex\">5^{2}</span>. However, <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{lcm}(a, b)</span> contains 3 factors of 5 , so <span class=\"tex\">a</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">5^{3}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">a b c d</span> contains 7 factors of 5 , it follows that two of <span class=\"tex\">b, c</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">d</span> are divisible by <span class=\"tex\">5^{2}</span>, and the other is not divisible by 5 . Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c, d)</span> is not divisible by 5 .</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c, d)</span> cannot contain any prime factors other than 2,3 , or 5 , it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b, c, d)=(\\text{C})\\boxed{3}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">\\left[x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots, x_{n}\\right]</span> denote the least common multiple of <span class=\"tex\">x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots, x_{n}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \\ldots, x_{n}\\right)</span> denote their greatest common divisor. Because <span class=\"tex\">[a, b, c]=[[a, b],[b, c]]</span> and <span class=\"tex\">[a, b, c, d]=[[a, b],[c, d]]</span> the following relations are satisfied.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n{[a, b, c] } &amp; =2^{3} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n{[b, c, d] } &amp; =2^{2} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n{[c, d, a] } &amp; =2^{3} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n{[d, a, b] } &amp; =2^{3} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3} \\\\\n{[a, b, c, d] } &amp; =2^{3} \\cdot 3^{3} \\cdot 5^{3}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>It now remains to appeal to the identity</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(a, b, c, d)=\\frac{a b c d[a, b, c][b, c, d][c, d, a][d, a, b]}{[a, b][a, c][a, d][b, c][b, d][c, d][a, b, c, d]}</span></p><p>This identity is a consequence of the identity</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; \\min (\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta)=\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma+\\delta \\\\\n&amp; \\quad-(\\max (\\alpha, \\beta)+\\max (\\alpha, \\gamma)+\\max (\\alpha, \\delta)+\\max (\\beta, \\gamma)+\\max (\\beta, \\delta)+\\max (\\gamma, \\delta)) \\\\\n&amp; \\quad+(\\max (\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma)+\\max (\\beta, \\gamma, \\delta)+\\max (\\gamma, \\delta, \\alpha)+\\max (\\delta, \\alpha, \\beta)) \\\\\n&amp; \\quad-\\max (\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>To prove this identity, observe that the variables are treated symmetrically, so that one may assume that <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha \\geq \\beta \\geq \\gamma \\geq \\delta</span>. Then the above asserts that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\delta=\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma+\\delta-(\\alpha+\\alpha+\\alpha+\\beta+\\beta+\\gamma)+(\\alpha+\\beta+\\alpha+\\alpha)-\\alpha</span></p><p>and this can be verified by simplifying. Applying the identity to the given values gives <span class=\"tex\">(a, b, c, d)=3</span>.\\<br/>\nNote: Here are several comments:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The pairwise lcm's determine all lcm's of <span class=\"tex\">k</span>-tuples for any <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, because</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left[a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots, a_{k}\\right]=\\left[\\left[a_{1}, a_{2}\\right],\\left[a_{2}, a_{3}\\right], \\ldots,\\left[a_{k-1}, a_{k}\\right]\\right]</span></p><ol start=\"2\">\n<li>Because the gcd is computed easily by the Euclidean Algorithm, it is more common to express an lcm in terms of gcd's. The formula</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">[a, b, c]=\\frac{a b c(a, b, c)}{(a, b)(b, c)(c, a)}</span></p><p>occurs in some texts in elementary number theory, but only very rarely.</p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>If <span class=\"tex\">A, B, C, D</span> are sets and <span class=\"tex\">I_{A}, I_{B}, I_{C}, I_{D}</span> are their indicator functions, then <span class=\"tex\">\\max \\left(I_{A}, I_{B}\\right)=I_{A \\cup B}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\min \\left(I_{A}, I_{B}\\right)=I_{A \\cap B}</span>. When <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha, \\ldots, \\delta</span> are taken to be these indicator functions in the identity above, it becomes Euler's Inclusion-Exclusion principle. When the same substitution is made in the corresponding identity found in the second solution, an expression is obtained for the intersection of several sets in terms of the unions of various subcollections. This is the set-theoretic dual of the Inclusion-Exclusion Principle.</li>\n</ol>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> A regular pentagon with area <span class=\"tex\">1+\\sqrt{5}</span> is printed on paper and cut out. All five vertices are folded to the center of the pentagon, creating a smaller pentagon. What is the area of the new pentagon?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">4-\\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{5}-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8-3 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2+\\sqrt{5}}{3}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>The folds are the perpendicular bisectors of the segments connecting the vertices of the pentagon to its center. Let <span class=\"tex\">C</span> be the center of the pentagon, let <span class=\"tex\">G, A</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">F</span> be three consecutive vertices in order, let <span class=\"tex\">D</span> be the intersection of the fold lines for vertices <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">F</span>, let <span class=\"tex\">B</span> be the intersection of the fold lines for vertices <span class=\"tex\">A</span> and <span class=\"tex\">G</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the other intersection of fold lines on the fold line for <span class=\"tex\">F</span>. Draw quadrilaterals <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A B E F</span>, as shown.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-103.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be the area of the small pentagon. Because the two pentagons are similar, the ratio of their areas is the square of the ratio of their side lengths. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{S}{1+\\sqrt{5}}=\\left(\\frac{B D}{A F}\\right)^{2}</span></p><p>By symmetry, <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> must be a rhombus, and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> bisects <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B A D</span> (and <span class=\"tex\">\\angle G A F</span> ). Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{gathered}\n\\angle B A D=\\angle B C D=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{5}=72^{\\circ} \\\\\n\\text { so } \\angle B A C=\\frac{72^{\\circ}}{2}=36^{\\circ} . \\text { Also, } \\angle G A F=\\frac{3 \\cdot 180^{\\circ}}{5}=108^{\\circ} \\text {, so } \\angle C A F=\\frac{108^{\\circ}}{2}=54^{\\circ} . \\text { Lastly, } \\\\\n\\angle B A F=\\angle B A C+\\angle C A F=36^{\\circ}+54^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ} .\n\\end{gathered}</span></p><p>Therefore quadrilateral <span class=\"tex\">A B E F</span> is a rectangle and <span class=\"tex\">B E=A F</span>. It is known (and proved below) that the ratio of a diagonal of a regular pentagon to its side is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}</span> ( <span class=\"tex\">\\varphi</span>, the golden ratio). Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{B D}{A F}=\\frac{B D}{B E}=\\frac{1}{\\varphi}=\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{5}},</span></p><p>and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{S}{1+\\sqrt{5}}=\\left(\\frac{B D}{A F}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2} .</span></p><p>The requested area is <span class=\"tex\">S=\\frac{2^{2}}{1+\\sqrt{5}}=(\\text{B})\\boxed{\\sqrt{5}-1}</span>.<br/>\nTo prove that the diagonal to side ratio for a regular pentagon equals <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}</span>, let regular pentagon PQRST have sides of length 1 and diagonals of length <span class=\"tex\">d</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">U</span> be the point of intersection of the line segments <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{P R}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{Q T}</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-104.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Because each diagonal is parallel to a side, parallelogram TURS is formed with sides of length 1, leaving segments <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{P U}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{Q U}</span> to have length <span class=\"tex\">d-1</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">P Q R T</span> is a trapezoid, <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle R T U</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle P Q U</span> are similar, and <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{d}{1}=\\frac{1}{d-1}</span>. This implies that <span class=\"tex\">d^{2}-d-1=0</span>, and the quadratic formula gives the positive solution <span class=\"tex\">d=\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\varphi</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Inscribe the regular pentagon in a circle of radius <span class=\"tex\">r</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">A</span> be the center of the circle, let <span class=\"tex\">C</span> be a vertex of the pentagon, and let <span class=\"tex\">B</span> be the midpoint of the minor arc from <span class=\"tex\">C</span> to one of the vertices adjacent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">D</span> be the intersection of the bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A B C</span> with <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">F</span> be the intersection of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> with the pentagon. Note that <span class=\"tex\">A F</span> is the center-to-side distance of the pentagon. See the figure.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2023B/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-105.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>The linear dimensions are reduced by the factor <span class=\"tex\">f=\\frac{r}{2 \\cdot A F}</span>, and the area is reduced by the factor <span class=\"tex\">f^{2}</span>. The scale factor <span class=\"tex\">f</span> can be evaluated using similar triangles. Triangle <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> is isosceles with angles <span class=\"tex\">36^{\\circ}, 72^{\\circ}, 72^{\\circ}</span> and sides <span class=\"tex\">A B=A C=r</span>. Segment <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B D}</span> bisects <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A B C</span> creating isosceles triangles <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B D</span> with two <span class=\"tex\">36^{\\circ}</span> angles and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B D C</span> with two <span class=\"tex\">72^{\\circ}</span> angles. Choose units so that <span class=\"tex\">1=B C=B D=A D</span>. Notice that <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B D C \\sim \\triangle A B C</span> and is smaller by a factor of <span class=\"tex\">r</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">A D+D C=A C</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">1+\\frac{1}{r}=r</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">r&gt;1</span>, this simplifies to <span class=\"tex\">r=\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}</span>. Now</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f=\\frac{A C}{2 \\cdot A F}=\\frac{A D}{2 \\cdot A E}=\\frac{1}{r}=\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{5}} .</span></p><p>The area of the smaller pentagon is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f^{2}(1+\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{4(1+\\sqrt{5})}{(1+\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=(\\text{B})\\boxed{\\sqrt{5}-1}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2023/part-b/problem-25"}]});
